inguishes surface and 0-200 m WOA01 geostrophy relative to 1500 dbar, modeled Sverdrup flow, and band-passed seasonal anomaly sections at 170 E. Anomaly zero contours and averaging windows cannot define current widths or endpoints. Numerical compatible flow fields remain needed.\",\r\n          \"source_url\": \"https://www.soest.hawaii.edu/oceanography/bo/CQ04.pdf\",\r\n          \"source_locator\": \"Figures 1, 2 and 8; paragraphs 2-3, 20 and 26.\",\r\n          \"observed_period\": null\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"evidence_links\": [],\r\n      \"object_url\": \"object.html?id=current%3Apacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent\",\r\n      \"route_url\": null,\r\n      \"season_url\": \"seasons.html?current=pacific-south-equatorial-countercurrent\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"id\": \"current:pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent\",\r\n      \"label\": \"Pacific South Subsurface Countercurrent\",\r\n      \"type\": \"named_current\",\r\n      \"identity_level\": \"current\",\r\n      \"source_kind\": \"eastward subsurface jets south of the Equatorial Undercurrent\",\r\n      \"setting\": \"equatorial\",\r\n      \"time_behavior\": \"persistent_variable\",\r\n      \"basin\": \"Equatorial South Pacific\",\r\n      \"capabilities\": {\r\n        \"observed_velocity\": 0,\r\n        \"radius_evidence\": 0,\r\n        \"reported_length\": 0,\r\n        \"reference_route\": 1,\r\n        \"scoped_width\": 1,\r\n        \"geometry\": 0,\r\n        \"time_samples\": 0,\r\n        \"source_connectivity\": 0,\r\n        \"scope_notes\": 1,\r\n        \"dated_diagnostics\": 0,\r\n        \"flow_network\": 0,\r\n        \"passage_transport\": 0\r\n      },\r\n      \"recorded_claims\": 56,\r\n      \"nasa_links\": 1,\r\n      \"fingerprint\": \"267c2910f3d0feb47aa794c52f95b1c2d2e6f93fb6a4845c4847b36261e890eb\",\r\n      \"series\": [],\r\n      \"section_fingerprints\": {\r\n        \"identity\": \"b7414bf443e8963a5b28ca403c015f93dda386b06a5e6fe57a874c0e5c244f15\",\r\n        \"sources\": \"b2df2a01e2a6474f8d1fd52dcb7abfe8f18d24b35f7879c657af589d4069e0a3\",\r\n        \"claims\": \"0e0c05a5d40952d9c2c97313b1d6ab731b38dead749fd38cf5c17ec5ff77e35f\",\r\n        \"measurements\": \"09913ea1ed86149f222febf291858e761589867e603167c0564914aca98c925b\",\r\n        \"routes_geometry\": \"a41b9a5f328318ad9e999375b059a9a2466c73f5345cac8c14d01462ef68b8b5\",\r\n        \"media\": \"58afbefd1dab0d7d6c48803e676a9db2cb525a9c221574435bb52c2a5c6e12b4\",\r\n        \"time_evidence\": \"8d86da8229951992c802d517eaeaeeb84a4b5f1a3e916ecd7944f448135387bd\"\r\n      },\r\n      \"latest_observation_date\": null,\r\n      \"claim_review_counts\": {\r\n        \"not_individually_reviewed\": 56\r\n      },\r\n      \"linked_source_count\": 3,\r\n      \"map_features\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"geometry\": {\r\n            \"coordinates\": [\r\n              -140,\r\n              -5.5\r\n            ],\r\n            \"type\": \"Point\"\r\n          },\r\n          \"role\": \"editorial_locator\",\r\n          \"note\": \"Editorial point between the two study-reported 140°W south-side jet branches at 4.5°S and 7°S; not an observed single-core position or whole-current route.\"\r\n        },\r\n        {\r\n          \"geometry\": {\r\n            \"type\": \"LineString\",\r\n            \"coordinates\": [\r\n              [\r\n                -155,\r\n                -3.5\r\n              ],\r\n              [\r\n                -140,\r\n                -4.2\r\n              ],\r\n              [\r\n                -125,\r\n                -4.8\r\n              ],\r\n              [\r\n                -110,\r\n                -5.5\r\n              ]\r\n            ]\r\n          },\r\n          \"role\": \"editorial_reference_route\",\r\n          \"note\": \"Declared historical southern mean-core studied reach from 155 W 3.5 S to 110 W 5.5 S. These gates truncate the larger southern subsurface flow. Western formation, secondary-jet geometry and eastern continuation are excluded. This is one study-defined connection, not an aggregate centerline or sum of primary and secondary branches.\"\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"state_evidence\": [],\r\n      \"connections\": [],\r\n      \"scope_notes\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"current_id\": \"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent\",\r\n          \"label\": \"Historical southern mean cores versus primary/secondary jets\",\r\n          \"audit_file\": \"research/pacific-sscc-mean-reach-branch-width-scope-audit.json\",\r\n          \"summary\": \"The 155 W to 110 W historical southern mean-core reach is one editorial connection, not a combined primary/secondary axis. Rowe et al. distinguish primary SSCC and secondary SSCC but leave independent origin versus splitting unresolved. Local 250 m/160 m mean cores do not define a uniform layer; transport integration bands and a roughly 40 km PV front are not current width or seasonal ranges. A separate Rowe et al. general 2-degree meridional width summary is now retained as approximately 220 km; the two jet records share one source claim, not independent observations or annual extrema.\",\r\n          \"source_url\": \"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml\",\r\n          \"source_locator\": \"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: south SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 S/250 m and 110 W 5.5 S/160 m. Latitude integration bands are not widths. Rowe et al. (2000), introduction and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption, distinguish primary and secondary southern jets.\",\r\n          \"observed_period\": null\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"evidence_links\": [],\r\n      \"object_url\": \"object.html?id=current%3Apacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent\",\r\n      \"route_url\": \"reference-routes.html#pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate\",\r\n      \"season_url\": \"seasons.html?current=pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent\"\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"id\": \"current:persian-gulf-saline-overflow\",\r\